    "content": "〔卷一・甲部(2)〕（7 分）\n\n一等差數列的首項為 $5$，公差為 $4$。\n(a) 求該數列的第 $20$ 項。（2 分）\n(b) 求該數列首 $20$ 項之和。（2 分）\n(c) 求最小的正整數 $n$，使該數列首 $n$ 項之和大於 $1000$。（3 分）",
    "explanation": "(c) 是卷一常見的「求最小 $n$」題型。可解二次不等式，亦可如上以試值收窄 —— 但無論用哪一種方法，都【必須同時展示 $n=21$ 不成立及 $n=22$ 成立】。只寫出 $n=22$ 而不驗證 $n=21$，等於未證明它是最小的，步驟分會被扣。這一點在等差、等比及複利題中同樣適用。",
    "referenceAnswer": "(a) $T_{20}=81$\n(b) $S_{20}=860$\n(c) $n=22$",
    "marks": 7,
    "referenceAnswerEn": "(a) $T_{20}=81$\n(b) $S_{20}=860$\n(c) $n=22$",
    "contentEn": "[Paper 1 · Section A(2)] (7 marks)\n\nThe first term of an arithmetic sequence is $5$ and the common difference is $4$.\n(a) Find the 20th term of the sequence. (2 marks)\n(b) Find the sum of the first 20 terms of the sequence. (2 marks)\n(c) Find the least positive integer $n$ such that the sum of the first $n$ terms exceeds $1000$. (3 marks)",
    "explanationEn": "Part (c) is the familiar \"find the least $n$\" type in Paper 1. The quadratic inequality may be solved, or values may be tried as above; but whichever method is used, both $n=21$ failing and $n=22$ succeeding must be shown. Writing $n=22$ without testing $n=21$ does not establish that it is the least value, and step marks are deducted. The same applies to questions on arithmetic and geometric sequences and on compound interest.",
    "markingScheme": "(a) $T_{n}=a+(n-1)d$，故 $T_{20}=5+19(4)$ …… 1 分\n　　$=81$ …… 1 分\n(b) $S_{20}=\\dfrac{20}{2}(a+T_{20})=10(5+81)$ …… 1 分\n　　$=860$ …… 1 分\n(c) $S_{n}=\\dfrac{n}{2}[2(5)+(n-1)(4)]=\\dfrac{n}{2}(4n+6)=n(2n+3)$ …… 1 分\n　　需 $2n^{2}+3n>1000$。當 $n=21$：$2(441)+63=945<1000$ …… 1 分\n　　當 $n=22$：$2(484)+66=1034>1000$，故最小的 $n$ 為 $22$ …… 1 分",
    "markingSchemeEn": "(a) $T_{n}=a+(n-1)d$, so $T_{20}=5+19(4)$ …… 1 mark\n    $=81$ …… 1 mark\n(b) $S_{20}=\\dfrac{20}{2}(a+T_{20})=10(5+81)$ …… 1 mark\n    $=860$ …… 1 mark\n(c) $S_{n}=\\dfrac{n}{2}[2(5)+(n-1)(4)]=\\dfrac{n}{2}(4n+6)=n(2n+3)$ …… 1 mark\n    Require $2n^{2}+3n>1000$. When $n=21$: $2(441)+63=945<1000$ …… 1 mark\n    When $n=22$: $2(484)+66=1034>1000$, so the least $n$ is $22$ …… 1 mark",
