    "contentEn": "[Paper 1 · Section B] (8 marks)\n\nA group of $4$ people is to be chosen from $4$ boys and $5$ girls.\n(a) Find the number of possible selections. (2 marks)\n(b) Find the number of selections consisting of exactly $2$ boys and $2$ girls. (3 marks)\n(c) If the group is chosen at random, find the probability that it contains at least $1$ boy. (3 marks)",
    "explanationEn": "This question demonstrates three judgements that must be kept distinct in problems on combinations. First, whether order matters: forming a group does not involve order, so combinations are used rather than permutations. Second, two independent choices must be multiplied and not added: in (b) the boys and the girls are chosen separately, so the two counts are multiplied. Third, (c) again demonstrates the complementary event: computing \"1 boy\", \"2 boys\", \"3 boys\" and \"4 boys\" directly requires four combinations to be found and added, whereas the complement is a single case, an enormous difference in work. Whenever \"at least\" appears, first check whether its opposite consists of only one or two cases.",
    "markingScheme": "(a) 從 $9$ 人中選 $4$ 人，不計次序：$C^{9}_{4}$ …… 1 分\n　　$=\\dfrac{9!}{4!\\,5!}=126$ …… 1 分\n(b) 男生：$C^{4}_{2}=6$ …… 1 分\n　　女生：$C^{5}_{2}=10$ …… 1 分\n　　由乘法原理：$6\\times10=60$ …… 1 分\n(c) 「至少 1 名男生」的餘事件為「全為女生」…… 1 分\n　　全為女生的選法：$C^{5}_{4}=5$，故 $P(\\text{全女})=\\dfrac{5}{126}$ …… 1 分\n　　$P(\\text{至少 1 男})=1-\\dfrac{5}{126}=\\dfrac{121}{126}$ …… 1 分",
    "markingSchemeEn": "(a) Choosing $4$ from $9$ people without regard to order: $C^{9}_{4}$ …… 1 mark\n    $=\\dfrac{9!}{4!\\,5!}=126$ …… 1 mark\n(b) Boys: $C^{4}_{2}=6$ …… 1 mark\n    Girls: $C^{5}_{2}=10$ …… 1 mark\n    By the multiplication principle: $6\\times10=60$ …… 1 mark\n(c) The complementary event of \"at least 1 boy\" is \"all girls\" …… 1 mark\n    Selections of all girls: $C^{5}_{4}=5$, so $P(\\text{all girls})=\\dfrac{5}{126}$ …… 1 mark\n    $P(\\text{at least 1 boy})=1-\\dfrac{5}{126}=\\dfrac{121}{126}$ …… 1 mark",
